WEBVTT

00:00:00.103 --> 00:00:05.655
This runnable example makes row multiplication
visible We join

00:00:05.681 --> 00:00:11.395
patients to admissions lab results and prescriptions
all by patient ID Each

00:00:11.421 --> 00:00:17.202
of those child tables can contain multiple rows for
one patient Run

00:00:17.228 --> 00:00:20.676
the query and focus on a patient with multiple
records in several

00:00:20.702 --> 00:00:24.862
child tables The joined row count can be much larger
than the

00:00:24.889 --> 00:00:30.763
individual event counts Notice the use of COUNT
DISTINCT for admission ID

00:00:30.934 --> 00:00:36.147
test ID and prescription ID Those distinct counts
recover the number of

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:40.568
original events while COUNT star counts the
combinations created by the join

00:00:42.315 --> 00:00:45.946
This is why COUNT star after a multi-child join can
be misleading

00:00:46.883 --> 00:00:49.409
It may be counting the shape of the join rather than
the

00:00:49.436 --> 00:00:54.771
business events you intended to measure The first
question after seeing an

00:00:54.797 --> 00:00:58.113
unexpectedly large count should be what is the grain
of the joined

00:00:58.139 --> 00:01:01.337
rows and which relationships are multiplying one
another
